{
  "slug": "artist:la-sonora-dinamita",
  "name": "La Sonora Dinamita",
  "editorial_article": "Formed in 1960 in Cartagena de Indias, Colombia, La Sonora Dinamita helped popularize cumbia across Latin America through the support of producer Don Antonio Fuentes. Under the direction of bandleader Lucho Argaín, the group recorded early work on the Discos Fuentes label before disbanding in 1963. In 1975, the ensemble was reactivated by Julio Ernesto Estrada 'Fruko' Rincón, transitioning to a Mexico City base by 1977. Their 1986 album, Sida, reached number five on the Regional Mexican Albums chart, featuring a ten-piece brass section that became the group's trademark. Over several decades, the band maintained a distinct sound anchored by female vocalists like Margarita Vargas, Mélida Yará, and Vilma Díaz, alongside hit recordings such as 'Mi Cucu', 'Se me perdió la cadenita', and 'El africano'. The group expanded its reach with a 1991 performance at Madison Square Garden, continuing to evolve its lineup following the 2002 passing of Lucho Argaín.",
